1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:09,000
They are icons of the open road and the backbone of the transportation and construction industries.

2
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:19,000
From off-road powerhouses to traveling condominiums, they are rigorously tested and built to last.

3
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Now, trucks on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,540
When the going gets tough, count on the world's arsenal

5
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:38,200
of wheeled workhorses to muscle in and meet the challenge.

6
00:00:43,340 --> 00:00:47,060
No matter how dirty or daunting the task,

7
00:00:47,160 --> 00:00:49,460
trucks ride to our rescue.

8
00:00:49,560 --> 00:00:52,760
From transporting our most dangerous cargoes,

9
00:00:52,860 --> 00:00:56,500
to carrying military and emergency teams,

10
00:00:56,501 --> 00:01:01,160
to supporting the mightiest civil engineering feats.

11
00:01:03,980 --> 00:01:07,780
But first and foremost, we rely on trucks to deliver.

12
00:01:08,860 --> 00:01:12,700
A network of multi-ton vehicles transporting goods and raw materials

13
00:01:12,701 --> 00:01:16,720
between producer and consumer is the economic lifeblood of the nation.

14
00:01:18,180 --> 00:01:22,100
More than 80% of all freight in the United States moves by truck.

15
00:01:22,101 --> 00:01:28,100
You know, as the economy grows, one of the things that takes off in parallel is truck driving.

16
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:35,480
Because this is the way that most goods are transported across the United States.

17
00:01:36,180 --> 00:01:40,700
In fact, pretty much anything you can think of has been brought to you at some point by a truck.

18
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:46,880
America's love affair with trucks is more than a century old and still going strong.

19
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:50,100
Trucks not only symbolize the romance of the open road,

20
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:55,280
But also, Thunder Along is the very epitome of power

21
00:01:55,281 --> 00:01:56,100
and endurance.

22
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:02,920
One recognizable phrase sums it up,

23
00:02:02,921 --> 00:02:05,640
built like a Mack truck.

24
00:02:05,641 --> 00:02:08,620
Mack trucks, now owned by Volvo North America,

25
00:02:08,621 --> 00:02:11,060
conjures images of ironclad strength

26
00:02:11,061 --> 00:02:12,380
and unwavering dependability.

27
00:02:15,120 --> 00:02:17,920
Volvo operates two test centers where researchers

28
00:02:17,921 --> 00:02:20,600
and engineers are helping to ensure that Mack maintains

29
00:02:20,601 --> 00:02:21,840
its rugged reputation.

30
00:02:21,841 --> 00:02:25,980
A truck customer expects a tremendous amount, and they should.

31
00:02:25,981 --> 00:02:31,520
In one year's time, a heavy-duty truck can run twice as many miles as your average car

32
00:02:31,521 --> 00:02:34,780
or pickup trucks will in an overall lifetime.

33
00:02:34,781 --> 00:02:40,940
Engineers at the test centers stress and load individual components to make sure they

34
00:02:40,941 --> 00:02:42,720
won't fail in the real world.

35
00:02:42,721 --> 00:02:49,160
With accelerometers, we gather direct information for calculating force on the components and

36
00:02:49,161 --> 00:02:55,060
assistance within the truck and we have strain gauges so that we can measure direct strain

37
00:02:55,061 --> 00:03:00,280
off the components and to be able to do stress analysis on these pieces.

38
00:03:00,281 --> 00:03:05,380
The cabling, as you see down the vehicle, will run into a data recording equipment so

39
00:03:05,381 --> 00:03:11,160
that we can take the real-time data later and extrapolate it and to be able to convert

40
00:03:11,161 --> 00:03:18,220
that electrical signal into direct measurements.

41
00:03:18,221 --> 00:03:22,440
Some of the machines seem more torture device than engineering equipment.

42
00:03:22,441 --> 00:03:29,340
The cab bump test rig is used to evaluate the durability of a cab under severe impact

43
00:03:29,341 --> 00:03:32,260
type of loads.

44
00:03:32,261 --> 00:03:37,780
This is particularly important for vocational type of trucks.

45
00:03:37,781 --> 00:03:42,340
As they operate off roads, you go through potholes, you go over rocks, bumps, various

46
00:03:42,341 --> 00:03:47,700
types of uneven terrain.

47
00:03:47,701 --> 00:03:52,700
Once the lab tests are completed, the trucks pit their strength against a half-mile gauntlet,

48
00:03:52,701 --> 00:03:57,020
where only the strong survive.

49
00:03:57,021 --> 00:04:01,460
The object is to see how well and how long they can withstand the worst road environment

50
00:04:01,461 --> 00:04:02,460
possible.

51
00:04:02,461 --> 00:04:08,740
On the endurance test, each truck that we have is tested for 12 weeks, roughly, which

52
00:04:08,741 --> 00:04:11,740
simulates a million kilometers of actual road miles.

53
00:04:11,741 --> 00:04:15,200
We run the test 24-7 until the test is completed.

54
00:04:15,201 --> 00:04:22,200
Now we're going to do a high speed lap at 24 miles an hour, run over the corrugations

55
00:04:22,201 --> 00:04:25,200
and that's basically to check the front suspension.

56
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:35,200
I can tell when something's not right, hear something mad noise like a brake in the suspension or something

57
00:04:35,201 --> 00:04:38,200
and I stop and check my vehicle to see what it was.

58
00:04:38,201 --> 00:04:45,060
Lessons learned at the test facilities ensure that today's Mack trucks live up to the reputation

59
00:04:45,061 --> 00:04:50,000
of their predecessors, which first rolled off the assembly line in 1905.

60
00:04:51,740 --> 00:04:55,740
Mack draws on decades of experience to build trucks for every application.

61
00:04:56,980 --> 00:05:02,160
Truck manufacturing is similar to automotive in that we have assembly lines, and that's

62
00:05:02,161 --> 00:05:02,861
where it ends.

63
00:05:02,862 --> 00:05:06,700
Henry Ford would roll over in his grave if he saw what was coming down our lines.

64
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:15,380
Yesteryear's auto pioneers would also be bowled over to see robots on the line, working side

65
00:05:15,381 --> 00:05:19,840
by side with workers to get dozens of trucks off the line every day.

66
00:05:19,841 --> 00:05:26,720
The Mack plant at the Mukunji facility just outside Allentown, Pennsylvania sprawls over

67
00:05:26,721 --> 00:05:32,380
a million square feet, enough floor space to house four aircraft carriers.

68
00:05:32,381 --> 00:05:41,380
The plant runs three cab lines, two chassis lines, and a paint facility.

69
00:05:41,381 --> 00:05:44,980
What we're looking at now is a frame.

70
00:05:44,981 --> 00:05:49,480
This is the bare bones truck right before anything really major happens to it.

71
00:05:49,481 --> 00:05:56,420
What the frame is made up of are major components of the frame rails, cross members, and then

72
00:05:56,421 --> 00:06:00,480
you have your front and rear axle assemblies.

73
00:06:00,481 --> 00:06:03,320
If these hit the line, we can do anything with these,

74
00:06:03,420 --> 00:06:05,480
and the truck can be 100 different variations

75
00:06:05,580 --> 00:06:08,720
on any set of rails like this.

76
00:06:08,820 --> 00:06:10,920
From these rails, workers can create vehicles

77
00:06:11,020 --> 00:06:14,920
ranging from garbage trucks to dumpers to mixers.

78
00:06:15,020 --> 00:06:17,540
The one thing they'll all have in common

79
00:06:17,640 --> 00:06:18,940
is a huge diesel engine.

80
00:06:22,140 --> 00:06:26,340
Some can produce an astonishing 600 horsepower.

81
00:06:26,440 --> 00:06:27,980
That's twice as much as that produced

82
00:06:27,981 --> 00:06:30,880
produced by an average pickup truck.

83
00:06:30,881 --> 00:06:35,180
These engines weigh in at a hefty 3,000 pounds.

84
00:06:35,181 --> 00:06:37,480
And even for experienced assembly line workers,

85
00:06:37,481 --> 00:06:42,220
placing them onto the frame is a difficult procedure.

86
00:06:42,221 --> 00:06:44,900
In another part of the factory, workers assemble the truck's

87
00:06:44,901 --> 00:06:45,601
cab.

88
00:06:48,640 --> 00:06:51,020
After welders make the initial welds,

89
00:06:51,021 --> 00:06:53,560
their robotic counterparts finish the job,

90
00:06:53,561 --> 00:06:55,860
doing the repetitive work of welding the cab's parts

91
00:06:55,861 --> 00:07:00,360
together.

92
00:07:00,361 --> 00:07:02,540
Once complete, the cab is hoisted over the frame

93
00:07:02,541 --> 00:07:05,700
and bolted into place.

94
00:07:05,701 --> 00:07:09,640
And the familiar form of the truck begins to take shape.

95
00:07:09,641 --> 00:07:12,180
Right now we're at the end of our H line in Mukunji.

96
00:07:12,181 --> 00:07:17,680
On this line daily, we have about 27 trucks come offline.

97
00:07:17,681 --> 00:07:20,360
They come off about every 15 minutes.

98
00:07:20,361 --> 00:07:22,980
Across our second chassis assembly line,

99
00:07:22,981 --> 00:07:27,540
We run that at about 39 a day, and they'll come off every nine minutes.

100
00:07:27,640 --> 00:07:31,340
So our overall rate between the two lines is 66 trucks a day.

101
00:07:32,660 --> 00:07:38,200
The vinyl product can average between $60,000 and $130,000 without the body.

102
00:07:40,220 --> 00:07:43,920
Once finished, the truck is ready to handle whatever the customer can dish out.

103
00:07:45,320 --> 00:07:50,720
But the strength and dependability of modern trucks is as much a story of evolution as of nuts and bolts.

104
00:07:52,980 --> 00:07:55,280
As far as the first truck in the world,

105
00:07:55,281 --> 00:07:58,020
there's some question about when that actually occurred.

106
00:07:58,021 --> 00:08:02,320
I think a lot has to do with what is the definition of a truck.

107
00:08:02,321 --> 00:08:05,760
There are records that indicate a French artillery captain

108
00:08:05,761 --> 00:08:09,460
by the name of Cunot developed a steam powered tractor

109
00:08:09,461 --> 00:08:15,100
used to pull heavy military cannons in 1769.

110
00:08:15,101 --> 00:08:16,700
Other large steam tractor engines

111
00:08:16,701 --> 00:08:19,300
would emerge and be used to pull buggies and wagons.

112
00:08:19,301 --> 00:08:24,140
But these early vehicles were inefficient and soon became

113
00:08:24,141 --> 00:08:25,540
novelties used by the wealthy.

114
00:08:27,540 --> 00:08:29,540
By the middle of the 19th century,

115
00:08:29,541 --> 00:08:32,540
the horse was still the most efficient form of road

116
00:08:32,541 --> 00:08:34,540
transportation.

117
00:08:34,541 --> 00:08:37,660
But that would change with the advent of the internal combustion

118
00:08:37,661 --> 00:08:41,660
engine by N.A. Otto in 1876.

119
00:08:44,460 --> 00:08:47,100
Over the next few years, tinkerers and craftsmen from

120
00:08:47,101 --> 00:08:52,100
and craftsmen from around the world embraced these new engines.

121
00:08:52,101 --> 00:08:56,100
Then in 1892, Charles Duryea built what many believed

122
00:08:56,101 --> 00:09:01,100
to be America's first gasoline-powered vehicle.

123
00:09:01,101 --> 00:09:05,100
Six years later, the Winton Motor Carriage Company of Cleveland

124
00:09:05,101 --> 00:09:07,100
claimed to build the first commercial gasoline delivery

125
00:09:07,101 --> 00:09:10,100
wagon, which was an accurate description.

126
00:09:13,100 --> 00:09:16,100
By the turn of the century, most truck bodies were little more

127
00:09:16,101 --> 00:09:18,000
were little more than modified wagons.

128
00:09:18,940 --> 00:09:19,880
Many were chain-driven,

129
00:09:20,420 --> 00:09:22,960
and some steered with a rudimentary device called a tiller.

130
00:09:24,860 --> 00:09:26,700
Adding to an already rough ride,

131
00:09:26,980 --> 00:09:28,720
the wheels were simple metal rims

132
00:09:28,721 --> 00:09:30,400
with a hard piece of rubber attached.

133
00:09:31,460 --> 00:09:33,260
A driver was asked to sit on a board,

134
00:09:33,380 --> 00:09:34,760
out in the weather, out in the rain,

135
00:09:35,480 --> 00:09:36,680
and deal with the elements.

136
00:09:37,660 --> 00:09:38,980
Driving in an early truck

137
00:09:38,981 --> 00:09:40,800
was not a very comfortable experience.

138
00:09:41,580 --> 00:09:42,900
I think it's important to recognize

139
00:09:42,901 --> 00:09:44,440
that when trucks were first developed

140
00:09:44,441 --> 00:09:46,220
that their focus was to haul freight,

141
00:09:46,500 --> 00:09:48,420
not necessarily provide driver comfort.

142
00:09:48,540 --> 00:09:50,080
And that's exactly what these vehicles did.

143
00:09:52,180 --> 00:09:54,580
To move freight, trucks needed power,

144
00:09:55,080 --> 00:09:57,380
something early gas engines had trouble providing.

145
00:09:58,920 --> 00:10:01,580
Railroads, by contrast, possessed more than enough power

146
00:10:01,581 --> 00:10:03,660
to continue their decades-long reign

147
00:10:03,661 --> 00:10:05,800
as the kings of long-distance transportation.

148
00:10:07,780 --> 00:10:11,160
But early gas-powered trucks faced their most immediate competition

149
00:10:11,161 --> 00:10:13,800
from trucks propelled by steam or electricity.

150
00:10:14,440 --> 00:10:18,340
When the truck was initially being developed, the very first trucks,

151
00:10:18,800 --> 00:10:23,100
it was not a foregone conclusion that they would be gasoline and then later diesel-powered.

152
00:10:23,280 --> 00:10:25,260
So there were electric trucks and steam trucks,

153
00:10:25,640 --> 00:10:30,420
and one of the problems with the early steam vehicles is that they were built like a locomotive.

154
00:10:30,421 --> 00:10:34,140
They had boilers, and they required multiple operators sometimes.

155
00:10:34,380 --> 00:10:38,420
You had to have a fuel source, and you had to keep it full of water, or it would explode.

156
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,620
The novel electric trucks were ahead of their time,

157
00:10:42,621 --> 00:10:44,980
but were encumbered by large batteries.

158
00:10:45,640 --> 00:10:48,280
Well, the same problems that we've had in the last 20 years

159
00:10:48,281 --> 00:10:50,400
trying to develop an exclusively electric car,

160
00:10:50,920 --> 00:10:53,140
the electric trucks had mammoth batteries.

161
00:10:53,340 --> 00:10:56,920
And recall, this is the battery technology of the early 20th century,

162
00:10:57,040 --> 00:10:58,820
giant lead batteries with lots of acid.

163
00:10:59,180 --> 00:11:00,840
Their upshot was that they were quiet,

164
00:11:00,841 --> 00:11:02,960
but for over-the-road use and general use,

165
00:11:03,500 --> 00:11:06,060
they just couldn't go very far without having to be recharged.

166
00:11:07,620 --> 00:11:09,720
For many consumers of the era, however,

167
00:11:09,840 --> 00:11:11,940
the hard choice wasn't gas or electric.

168
00:11:12,620 --> 00:11:14,940
It was car or horse.

169
00:11:15,840 --> 00:11:18,820
Eventually it became very clear that gasoline-powered trucks

170
00:11:18,821 --> 00:11:21,140
are cheaper to operate than horses and wagons.

171
00:11:21,560 --> 00:11:23,220
You can park a truck for two or three days,

172
00:11:23,420 --> 00:11:25,400
you don't need to feed it, you don't need to watch it,

173
00:11:25,620 --> 00:11:28,080
it doesn't get a cold, it doesn't get sick, it doesn't die.

174
00:11:28,620 --> 00:11:31,940
It does need repair, but it was just cheaper in the long run

175
00:11:31,941 --> 00:11:32,960
than horses and wagons.

176
00:11:34,300 --> 00:11:36,820
Although horse-drawn wagons struggled to compete

177
00:11:36,821 --> 00:11:38,440
against gasoline-powered vehicles,

178
00:11:39,220 --> 00:11:41,960
the Teamsters who drove them weren't going down without a fight.

179
00:11:42,620 --> 00:11:50,080
And one of the ways that the Teamsters would try to get a leg up is by backing their wagons with the horses into the radiator of trucks.

180
00:11:50,320 --> 00:11:54,160
That was a way to disarm the truck because that was a very delicate part of the truck at the time.

181
00:11:56,400 --> 00:12:01,500
Although heavy, slow, and crude, the fledgling trucks had proven they were here to stay.

182
00:12:02,340 --> 00:12:08,960
In years to come, innovators would develop a sleek new fleet of workhorses that would revolutionize the industry.

183
00:12:08,961 --> 00:12:16,412
In 1919, a military truck convoy took 62 days to travel from Washington, D.C.

184
00:12:16,413 --> 00:12:17,960
to San Francisco

185
00:12:17,961 --> 00:12:21,580
because of poor roads and mechanical breakdowns.

186
00:12:22,740 --> 00:12:27,440
Trucks will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

187
00:12:33,520 --> 00:12:38,520
Make room for these earth-moving behemoths that stand more than two stories tall.

188
00:12:38,960 --> 00:12:41,800
Drivers must scale ladders at the front of the vehicle

189
00:12:41,900 --> 00:12:44,500
to reach the cab.

190
00:12:44,600 --> 00:12:47,700
Once there, they can literally move mountains.

191
00:12:47,800 --> 00:12:51,900
Trucks like these are able to haul 320 tons of earth.

192
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:57,020
Fully loaded, they weigh in at over a million pounds.

193
00:12:57,120 --> 00:13:02,820
And every ounce of that weight rests solely on the tires.

194
00:13:02,920 --> 00:13:06,420
Made of special compounds of rubber and synthetic material,

195
00:13:06,421 --> 00:13:09,420
The tires are marvels unto themselves.

196
00:13:09,520 --> 00:13:12,020
They stand over 12 and 1⁄2 feet tall

197
00:13:12,120 --> 00:13:14,020
and weigh up to five tons.

198
00:13:17,360 --> 00:13:19,460
Today's trucks, no matter what their size,

199
00:13:19,560 --> 00:13:23,680
owe their existence to the tires they ride on.

200
00:13:23,780 --> 00:13:25,980
And although modern tires are built to withstand

201
00:13:26,080 --> 00:13:28,180
the most punishing road conditions,

202
00:13:28,280 --> 00:13:33,520
blowouts are a fact of life, caused either by road debris

203
00:13:33,521 --> 00:13:36,900
debris or excess friction and heat.

204
00:13:36,901 --> 00:13:40,060
The best truck tires deliver not only when fully inflated,

205
00:13:40,061 --> 00:13:42,840
but also when they're punctured.

206
00:13:42,841 --> 00:13:47,980
Case in point, Michelin's new super-wide tire.

207
00:13:47,981 --> 00:13:51,760
The construction of the tire is such that when you have a blowout,

208
00:13:51,761 --> 00:13:53,960
and we've done this with dynamite on the tires,

209
00:13:53,961 --> 00:13:58,180
basically plastic explosive, you can see that it'll just

210
00:13:58,181 --> 00:13:58,881
sit right down.

211
00:13:58,882 --> 00:14:00,360
The truck will come to a safe stop.

212
00:14:03,520 --> 00:14:07,160
But the most manageable blowout of all is the one you can see coming.

213
00:14:08,700 --> 00:14:12,900
Volvo North America's advanced engineering team is working on a new monitoring system

214
00:14:12,901 --> 00:14:15,700
that will enable drivers to keep tabs on their tires.

215
00:14:16,600 --> 00:14:21,160
With these devices, we know the pressure in this tire even going down the road.

216
00:14:21,280 --> 00:14:24,490
If a driver were to pick up a nail, that's not going to get better.

217
00:14:24,491 --> 00:14:25,400
It only gets worse.

218
00:14:25,920 --> 00:14:30,300
So the driver is notified in the cab you have a leak in this tire.

219
00:14:30,301 --> 00:14:32,740
but at the same time he's getting that signal,

220
00:14:33,240 --> 00:14:35,660
we can broadcast that signal through satellite

221
00:14:35,661 --> 00:14:39,600
and go to a service, third-party service or the fleet itself,

222
00:14:40,120 --> 00:14:43,680
and say, okay, this driver needs this tire, he's in this truck,

223
00:14:43,760 --> 00:14:45,300
we know where he is, we know where he's going.

224
00:14:46,660 --> 00:14:49,780
The advanced system will be a cost-effective time saver

225
00:14:49,781 --> 00:14:52,740
as a new tire can be ready at a predetermined site

226
00:14:52,741 --> 00:14:54,940
before the troubled truck suffers a blowout.

227
00:14:54,941 --> 00:15:04,100
A century ago, the earliest trucks were equipped with tires that were impervious to blowouts,

228
00:15:04,101 --> 00:15:05,800
but had little else going for them.

229
00:15:05,801 --> 00:15:10,120
The first trucks mostly used solid rubber tires.

230
00:15:10,121 --> 00:15:14,880
And this was fine as long as the top speeds were limited to about 10 miles an hour.

231
00:15:14,881 --> 00:15:17,220
And that was good for city use.

232
00:15:17,221 --> 00:15:20,940
If you went out on dirt roads in the country, you had very poor traction with these kinds

233
00:15:20,941 --> 00:15:24,720
of tires, no tread or anything.

234
00:15:24,721 --> 00:15:31,000
In the early 19th century, air-filled or pneumatic tires were commonly used on bicycles and early

235
00:15:31,001 --> 00:15:35,680
passenger cars, but they were not well suited for early trucks.

236
00:15:35,681 --> 00:15:43,540
A truck with solid rubber tires could carry as much as the driver dared to load.

237
00:15:43,541 --> 00:15:47,240
But early air-filled tires would explode under too much weight.

238
00:15:47,241 --> 00:15:52,120
Even as tire technology advanced, most drivers and owners remained skeptical about the durability

239
00:15:52,121 --> 00:15:54,400
of pneumatic tires.

240
00:15:54,401 --> 00:16:00,020
They had a good product, but the public was not convinced because many of the companies

241
00:16:00,021 --> 00:16:06,100
that had large trucks and large heavy freight had never had any problems with the solid

242
00:16:06,101 --> 00:16:08,640
tire on the rim and the wood spoke.

243
00:16:08,641 --> 00:16:11,360
It was just a well-worked horse.

244
00:16:11,361 --> 00:16:15,280
So why should they buy these new rubber tires?

245
00:16:15,281 --> 00:16:18,260
Despite their shortcomings, trucks were catching on.

246
00:16:18,261 --> 00:16:25,260
At the outset of World War I, the British and French armies contracted American manufacturers,

247
00:16:25,261 --> 00:16:32,760
such as FWD and White Motors, to build trucks for the war effort.

248
00:16:32,761 --> 00:16:35,394
Years before the arrival of American troops, U.S.

249
00:16:35,395 --> 00:16:37,760
trucks would serve on Europe's battlefields.

250
00:16:37,761 --> 00:16:39,760
These trucks were shipped overseas.

251
00:16:39,761 --> 00:16:43,660
It turned out the trucks weren't too good using in the front lines, because they were

252
00:16:43,661 --> 00:16:46,100
bogged down in the mud of the trenches.

253
00:16:46,101 --> 00:16:50,900
But they were wonderful behind the lines for shipping supplies up to the soldiers in the

254
00:16:50,901 --> 00:16:51,900
front lines.

255
00:16:51,901 --> 00:16:56,020
Legends were born.

256
00:16:56,021 --> 00:17:01,820
The Mack Model AC, with its pug nose and reputation for stubborn reliability, was nicknamed the

257
00:17:01,821 --> 00:17:05,360
Bulldog by grateful British troops.

258
00:17:05,361 --> 00:17:12,300
The Bulldog nickname would stick and later become the company's trademark.

259
00:17:12,301 --> 00:17:15,180
from the battlefields of Europe, the American military

260
00:17:15,181 --> 00:17:16,980
would soon be putting trucks to the test.

261
00:17:18,600 --> 00:17:23,620
In March 1916, U.S. soldiers led by General John J. Pershing

262
00:17:23,621 --> 00:17:27,380
crossed the border into Mexico in pursuit of Pancho Villa,

263
00:17:27,700 --> 00:17:29,500
who had attacked Columbus, New Mexico.

264
00:17:34,360 --> 00:17:37,960
Included in the strike force, 550 trucks.

265
00:17:37,961 --> 00:17:44,500
Unfortunately, the U.S. Army had trained neither drivers nor mechanics

266
00:17:44,501 --> 00:17:46,560
and was forced to hire civilians.

267
00:17:48,920 --> 00:17:55,680
To make matters worse, numerous manufacturers had built the 128 different makes and models in the convoy.

268
00:17:59,020 --> 00:18:04,340
Supplying thousands of non-interchangeable parts to keep the trucks moving was almost impossible.

269
00:18:04,341 --> 00:18:10,340
Still, Pershing recognized the potential benefit of military trucks.

270
00:18:10,341 --> 00:18:18,200
In 1917, he already had learned a valuable lesson in Mexico, and he immediately went

271
00:18:18,201 --> 00:18:23,720
to Washington and to the Bureau of Standards and said, hey, we have to come up with a standardized

272
00:18:23,721 --> 00:18:29,140
truck because the problems we had in Mexico, we can't repeat in Europe.

273
00:18:29,141 --> 00:18:33,460
When American troops were deployed in 1917,

274
00:18:33,461 --> 00:18:37,240
the U.S. military hastily ordered 100,000 trucks.

275
00:18:38,720 --> 00:18:42,580
The government contracted more than 200 companies to build the vehicles.

276
00:18:43,680 --> 00:18:47,480
But since the problem of interchangeable parts still hadn't been solved,

277
00:18:48,020 --> 00:18:49,980
it was another recipe for disaster.

278
00:18:51,060 --> 00:18:52,560
Something had to be done.

279
00:18:53,800 --> 00:18:58,800
The Army called together several of the heavy-duty truck manufacturers

280
00:18:58,801 --> 00:19:01,780
and engine manufacturers, transmission suppliers,

281
00:19:02,020 --> 00:19:04,960
to come up with a standard truck design

282
00:19:04,961 --> 00:19:06,800
that they could all build at the same time

283
00:19:06,801 --> 00:19:08,120
and parts would be interchangeable.

284
00:19:08,620 --> 00:19:12,740
And this was officially called the standard Type B,

285
00:19:13,220 --> 00:19:16,440
but it's generally referred to as the Liberty Truck.

286
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:18,820
World War I was a very significant event

287
00:19:18,821 --> 00:19:21,260
as far as the development of the trucking industry is concerned.

288
00:19:21,480 --> 00:19:23,040
If we simply look at the numbers,

289
00:19:23,860 --> 00:19:26,400
in approximately 1908 there were 4,000 trucks

290
00:19:26,401 --> 00:19:27,560
in use in the United States.

291
00:19:27,561 --> 00:19:30,480
Before World War I, it had gone to 300,000,

292
00:19:30,980 --> 00:19:33,820
and after World War I, it had exploded to over a million trucks.

293
00:19:34,400 --> 00:19:37,140
The trucks were able to prove themselves to the American public

294
00:19:37,141 --> 00:19:40,640
that they were capable of carrying heavy loads under very difficult conditions.

295
00:19:43,240 --> 00:19:47,000
American manufacturers looked to cash in on the truck's newfound acceptance.

296
00:19:49,180 --> 00:19:50,840
For the Goodyear Tire Company,

297
00:19:50,980 --> 00:19:54,240
this meant finally selling the public on improved pneumatic tires.

298
00:19:54,241 --> 00:19:58,280
In 1918, to show the product's superiority,

299
00:19:59,420 --> 00:20:01,820
Goodyear outfitted seven trucks with the new tires

300
00:20:01,821 --> 00:20:03,900
and set them loose on America's roads.

301
00:20:05,800 --> 00:20:08,500
The pneumatic tires allowed for increased speed

302
00:20:08,501 --> 00:20:10,140
and better traction off-road,

303
00:20:10,800 --> 00:20:14,360
a crucial improvement as the American road system was poorly developed.

304
00:20:16,140 --> 00:20:18,460
The roads that connected cities and states

305
00:20:18,461 --> 00:20:21,640
were little more than dirt paths, if they existed at all.

306
00:20:21,641 --> 00:20:27,900
Trucks would bounce off the treacherous roads or smash through bridges built to handle horse traffic.

307
00:20:28,640 --> 00:20:34,900
Road conditions and tires are what limited the sizes of the trucks as they grew.

308
00:20:35,980 --> 00:20:42,840
I think that truck technology was out running ahead of the road conditions in this country.

309
00:20:44,900 --> 00:20:48,140
Still, the trucks were increasing in power and dependability,

310
00:20:48,141 --> 00:20:51,700
and the booming post-war economy was ready to put them to work.

311
00:20:52,460 --> 00:20:57,140
So after the war, there was a pent-up demand for goods in the country,

312
00:20:57,820 --> 00:20:59,300
a poor transportation system,

313
00:20:59,500 --> 00:21:02,580
and thousands and thousands of soldiers coming back with the idea,

314
00:21:02,780 --> 00:21:03,920
I'm going to start a truck line.

315
00:21:05,260 --> 00:21:08,760
But one vehicle manufacturer was conspicuously absent.

316
00:21:10,940 --> 00:21:13,900
Originally, Henry Ford didn't see the need to build trucks.

317
00:21:13,901 --> 00:21:20,040
He was obsessed with mass producing and selling his Model T automobiles cheaply.

318
00:21:20,640 --> 00:21:22,420
Another vehicle line would just interfere.

319
00:21:24,700 --> 00:21:29,260
Wagon makers, carpenters, blacksmiths, and even other truck manufacturers

320
00:21:29,261 --> 00:21:33,800
made good money converting Ford's Model T cars into extended bed trucks.

321
00:21:35,720 --> 00:21:40,440
While some industry giants slumbered, others saw the potential for great profit.

322
00:21:40,441 --> 00:21:52,260
In 1900, the Firestone Company, which was competing with arch-rival Goodyear to put tires on American vehicles, began manufacturing in Akron, Ohio.

323
00:21:55,920 --> 00:21:59,480
Firestone made a fortune selling tires to Ford and other manufacturers.

324
00:22:01,120 --> 00:22:07,380
In 1919, Firestone initiated the Ship by Truck campaign in order to promote long-haul trucking.

325
00:22:10,440 --> 00:22:14,000
As trucks began to loosen the railroad's grip on transportation,

326
00:22:15,020 --> 00:22:18,140
improved technology gave the growing industry an added boost.

327
00:22:19,640 --> 00:22:22,660
Prior to the 1920s, the engines that were found in trucks

328
00:22:22,661 --> 00:22:26,320
were two-cylinders and four-cylinders, gas-powered, with limited horsepower.

329
00:22:27,340 --> 00:22:30,660
With the need to carry heavier loads over improved roads,

330
00:22:30,940 --> 00:22:32,800
there was a need to have more powerful engines.

331
00:22:33,400 --> 00:22:36,900
We started seeing a movement from four-cylinder engines to inline six-cylinder engines,

332
00:22:37,300 --> 00:22:39,660
which is pretty much the standard in today's heavy-duty trucks.

333
00:22:40,440 --> 00:22:49,820
The vehicles began to look like modern trucks, with covered cabs for the driver and goods hauled in trailers as opposed to being strapped to the vehicle's body.

334
00:22:51,640 --> 00:22:58,580
By 1927, the number of American trucks had grown to nearly 3 million, and demand was increasing daily.

335
00:23:00,620 --> 00:23:08,720
Numerous truck manufacturers were there to fill the orders, but the good times were about to end, as the Great Depression took its toll on the economy.

336
00:23:08,721 --> 00:23:14,020
When the market crashed and the demand for trucks ceased,

337
00:23:14,780 --> 00:23:20,180
most of them weren't in the financial position to weather the storm that followed.

338
00:23:22,420 --> 00:23:25,700
Despite the Depression, however, some truck companies benefited.

339
00:23:26,820 --> 00:23:30,360
Public works projects from road construction to the building of Hoover Dam

340
00:23:30,361 --> 00:23:33,880
depended on powerful trucks to haul tons of earth and equipment.

341
00:23:33,881 --> 00:23:42,340
names like Kenworth, Sterling and Mack remain strong and continue to keep up with technology

342
00:23:42,341 --> 00:23:50,480
German engineer Rudolf Diesel revolutionized the industry by inventing the efficient engine

343
00:23:50,481 --> 00:23:56,860
that still bears his name diesel's idea of using the compression of the engine to cause the

344
00:23:56,861 --> 00:24:03,100
explosion of the fuel was the turning point in the development of the so-called diesel

345
00:24:03,101 --> 00:24:04,520
internal combustion engine.

346
00:24:05,640 --> 00:24:07,240
Unlike gasoline models,

347
00:24:07,840 --> 00:24:09,540
diesel engines don't rely on a spark

348
00:24:09,541 --> 00:24:10,360
to ignite the fuel.

349
00:24:11,480 --> 00:24:13,420
Instead, the engine compresses the air

350
00:24:13,421 --> 00:24:15,140
and the diesel mixture so forcefully

351
00:24:15,141 --> 00:24:17,920
that the fuel destabilizes and combusts

352
00:24:17,921 --> 00:24:19,100
because of the high compression

353
00:24:19,101 --> 00:24:20,180
in the combustion chamber.

354
00:24:22,060 --> 00:24:23,560
The blast is much greater

355
00:24:23,561 --> 00:24:24,540
than in the gas engine.

356
00:24:25,360 --> 00:24:27,340
This means the diesel produces more power,

357
00:24:27,900 --> 00:24:29,900
but it also means that all engine pieces

358
00:24:29,901 --> 00:24:31,420
must be larger and stronger

359
00:24:31,421 --> 00:24:32,860
to withstand the punishment.

360
00:24:33,100 --> 00:24:41,860
The greater power output was a boon to the industry, but the heavier built engines were

361
00:24:41,861 --> 00:24:43,340
more expensive to make.

362
00:24:43,341 --> 00:24:49,420
It would be years before they became standard equipment.

363
00:24:49,421 --> 00:24:54,660
With the outbreak of World War II, dependable manufacturers like Mack and General Motors

364
00:24:54,661 --> 00:25:00,200
devoted all their resources to building military trucks.

365
00:25:00,201 --> 00:25:04,300
The Army's new workhorse was the 2.5-ton dual axle,

366
00:25:04,301 --> 00:25:07,540
which would move troops and supplies in Europe, Africa,

367
00:25:07,541 --> 00:25:11,540
and throughout the Pacific theater.

368
00:25:11,541 --> 00:25:14,420
At war's end, North American manufacturers

369
00:25:14,421 --> 00:25:19,880
had built 2,600,887 trucks for victory overseas.

370
00:25:23,820 --> 00:25:27,700
By the 1950s, a new highway system and a booming economy

371
00:25:27,701 --> 00:25:30,040
laid the groundwork for the golden age of trucking.

372
00:25:32,940 --> 00:25:36,640
In 1931, mechanic inventor Klessie L. Cummins

373
00:25:36,641 --> 00:25:40,300
drove coast to coast in a truck with a Cummins diesel engine

374
00:25:40,301 --> 00:25:45,220
on a mere $11.22 worth of fuel.

375
00:25:45,221 --> 00:25:47,660
Trucks will return on Modern Marvels.

376
00:25:47,661 --> 00:25:56,660
The open road.

377
00:25:59,660 --> 00:26:02,660
Shimmering heat waves rise off endless miles of blacktop.

378
00:26:05,560 --> 00:26:09,660
A solitary driver pilots a lonely, austere 18-wheeler.

379
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:15,200
The image of the monotonous life of a trucker

380
00:26:15,201 --> 00:26:18,200
that today's truck manufacturers would like to make obsolete.

381
00:26:21,100 --> 00:26:22,200
The biggest names in the industry

382
00:26:22,300 --> 00:26:24,200
are pushing the envelope of design

383
00:26:24,300 --> 00:26:26,200
to build trucks with world-class comfort.

384
00:26:30,140 --> 00:26:33,200
Today's cockpits are high-tech and luxurious.

385
00:26:33,300 --> 00:26:36,200
This is a 77-inch sleeper, which is one of the largest

386
00:26:36,300 --> 00:26:38,200
production sleepers in the industry.

387
00:26:38,300 --> 00:26:40,200
This sleeper features a lot of amenities

388
00:26:40,300 --> 00:26:42,200
that make this a very comfortable home environment

389
00:26:42,201 --> 00:26:43,740
environment for the driver.

390
00:26:43,840 --> 00:26:47,100
A nice upper bunk with an interspring mattress.

391
00:26:47,200 --> 00:26:49,500
The lower bunk also features a workstation,

392
00:26:49,600 --> 00:26:52,240
which is a nice area for dining or working

393
00:26:52,340 --> 00:26:53,440
at the end of the day.

394
00:26:53,540 --> 00:26:56,540
So all in all, the driver has a lot of place to store items,

395
00:26:56,640 --> 00:26:58,240
a very comfortable place to stay at night.

396
00:27:07,020 --> 00:27:11,300
While such customized comfort is routine today,

397
00:27:11,301 --> 00:27:17,380
In the 1940s, driver comfort was low on the list of the trucking industry's priorities.

398
00:27:21,420 --> 00:27:26,100
After World War II, its number one goal was to meet escalating consumer demand.

399
00:27:27,580 --> 00:27:30,580
Trucks began traveling faster and farther than ever before.

400
00:27:34,760 --> 00:27:38,560
They could easily handle the flat terrain in the eastern half of the United States.

401
00:27:39,400 --> 00:27:42,560
But the mountains in the rugged west were a different story.

402
00:27:43,340 --> 00:27:47,860
The 275-horsepower supercharged Cummins in this 1955 Kenworth

403
00:27:47,861 --> 00:27:50,620
was one of the most powerful diesel engines available at the time.

404
00:27:52,780 --> 00:27:57,000
On long grades and steep hills, some of the lesser horsepower trucks,

405
00:27:57,080 --> 00:27:58,780
the 150 and 200-horsepower trucks,

406
00:27:58,940 --> 00:28:02,040
you'd practically be counting the lug nuts as they rotated around.

407
00:28:02,100 --> 00:28:03,380
You could walk beside the truck.

408
00:28:03,381 --> 00:28:05,020
It would crawl up the hill so slowly.

409
00:28:05,021 --> 00:28:09,600
A handful of Western truck manufacturers

410
00:28:09,601 --> 00:28:11,480
were trying to bring the West up to speed.

411
00:28:16,420 --> 00:28:18,700
The owners of a Utah shipping company

412
00:28:18,701 --> 00:28:20,240
named Consolidated Freight

413
00:28:20,241 --> 00:28:23,120
figured success hinged on lighter trailers

414
00:28:23,121 --> 00:28:25,060
and more space to haul more goods.

415
00:28:27,560 --> 00:28:30,020
Unable to find a manufacturer to meet their needs,

416
00:28:30,460 --> 00:28:32,060
they decided to build their own vehicles.

417
00:28:32,061 --> 00:28:35,560
and the Freightliner Corporation was born.

418
00:28:38,100 --> 00:28:40,840
It became a mainstay of West Coast trucking.

419
00:28:41,360 --> 00:28:44,400
But such new companies equipped with improving technology

420
00:28:44,401 --> 00:28:47,820
could only go so far on America's underdeveloped road system.

421
00:28:49,260 --> 00:28:53,880
The highway system is the partner with trucks in trucking.

422
00:28:54,340 --> 00:28:56,880
And early roads were just impassable at times.

423
00:28:57,080 --> 00:28:59,000
Mud ruts passed for roads.

424
00:28:59,001 --> 00:29:01,760
And if you couldn't get through, the freight couldn't get through.

425
00:29:02,060 --> 00:29:04,860
And it's been a gradual process of roads being improved

426
00:29:04,861 --> 00:29:07,460
to improve our national transportation system.

427
00:29:08,040 --> 00:29:11,600
In 1956, President Dwight D. Eisenhower

428
00:29:11,601 --> 00:29:13,660
signed the Federal Aid Highway Act,

429
00:29:14,300 --> 00:29:18,020
which authorized the creation of a 41,000-mile interstate system.

430
00:29:21,240 --> 00:29:24,660
Originally designed to accommodate military equipment convoys,

431
00:29:25,200 --> 00:29:26,940
the interstate system was ideal

432
00:29:26,941 --> 00:29:29,340
for large multi-trailered commercial trucks.

433
00:29:29,341 --> 00:29:35,320
And a lot more thought was put into on-ramps, off-ramps,

434
00:29:35,540 --> 00:29:38,080
overpasses, bridges, that sort of thing,

435
00:29:38,260 --> 00:29:41,080
just to make sure that trucks could effectively

436
00:29:41,081 --> 00:29:42,560
and safely carry their loads.

437
00:29:44,300 --> 00:29:47,720
The golden age of American trucking was in full swing.

438
00:29:49,640 --> 00:29:51,840
Better roads and more powerful trucks

439
00:29:51,841 --> 00:29:54,300
enabled the industry to deliver almost anything

440
00:29:54,301 --> 00:29:57,040
to any location, quickly and affordably.

441
00:29:57,041 --> 00:30:03,600
For the first time ever, trucks overtook railroads as the predominant way to ship goods.

442
00:30:04,740 --> 00:30:10,820
By the mid-1960s, more than 13 million commercial vehicles were traversing America's roads.

443
00:30:12,840 --> 00:30:18,520
Many sported the latest in truck technology, lighter aluminum and fiberglass trailers.

444
00:30:20,340 --> 00:30:25,400
But when the 70s arrived, improvements in engine design would take center stage

445
00:30:25,401 --> 00:30:28,140
as the world's fuel supplies were threatened.

446
00:30:30,160 --> 00:30:33,200
Most 18-wheelers today have 12 gears,

447
00:30:33,760 --> 00:30:35,780
10 forward and 2 reverse.

448
00:30:36,860 --> 00:30:39,500
Trucks will return on Modern Marvels.

449
00:30:45,300 --> 00:30:47,360
They are the kings of the road,

450
00:30:48,100 --> 00:30:49,920
commanding 40 tons of machinery,

451
00:30:50,540 --> 00:30:54,040
moving at 70 miles per hour along endless ribbons of asphalt.

452
00:30:55,400 --> 00:30:59,040
But even the best drivers are vulnerable to the hazards of the open road.

453
00:31:01,700 --> 00:31:06,960
Perhaps no company recognizes the inevitability of traffic mishaps more than Volvo.

454
00:31:08,100 --> 00:31:13,000
It's built its reputation building safe, reliable vehicles, including trucks.

455
00:31:19,760 --> 00:31:22,540
At Volvo's safety testing center in Sweden,

456
00:31:23,400 --> 00:31:27,300
engineers orchestrate a symphony of sudden impacts in the name of safety.

457
00:31:32,000 --> 00:31:36,460
They crash vehicles into fixed objects to judge drivers' survivability

458
00:31:36,461 --> 00:31:39,360
and learn how to improve overall truck design.

459
00:31:40,360 --> 00:31:43,100
One of the drivers' concerns that we have to address is,

460
00:31:43,101 --> 00:31:46,340
what happens with that engine that's directly in front of you in an accident?

461
00:31:46,900 --> 00:31:51,160
So we've designed that engine to move down and underneath the cab,

462
00:31:51,161 --> 00:31:55,360
thereby allowing the driver to safely exit the vehicle

463
00:31:55,361 --> 00:31:57,260
when the accident is completed.

464
00:31:59,100 --> 00:32:02,720
Today, 50% of truck driver fatalities in the U.S.

465
00:32:02,721 --> 00:32:04,460
involve a rollover crash.

466
00:32:05,520 --> 00:32:07,400
To test a cab's integrity,

467
00:32:08,340 --> 00:32:10,600
technicians slam one metric ton weights

468
00:32:10,601 --> 00:32:13,840
into window support columns in the rear of the cab

469
00:32:13,841 --> 00:32:17,220
to simulate the crushing forces exerted during an accident.

470
00:32:18,320 --> 00:32:20,760
The tests have yielded better cab design

471
00:32:20,761 --> 00:32:22,960
and construction.

472
00:32:22,961 --> 00:32:25,820
The knowledge gained in thorough research and testing

473
00:32:25,821 --> 00:32:27,360
has paid off in lives saved.

474
00:32:29,900 --> 00:32:33,240
Truck driver fatality rates have dropped roughly 50%

475
00:32:33,241 --> 00:32:36,620
over the last 20 years.

476
00:32:36,621 --> 00:32:39,580
But truck safety is as much about preventing accidents

477
00:32:39,581 --> 00:32:40,420
as surviving them.

478
00:32:43,360 --> 00:32:46,160
Anti-lock brake systems, or ABS,

479
00:32:46,161 --> 00:32:49,700
have improved with the addition of computer monitoring,

480
00:32:49,701 --> 00:32:53,200
helping to bring multi-ton vehicles to a stop quickly and safely.

481
00:32:54,460 --> 00:32:57,980
When you think about the size of a tractor trailer,

482
00:32:57,981 --> 00:33:03,100
you don't realize sometimes that the most critical part of that tractor

483
00:33:03,101 --> 00:33:05,720
is this right here, where it hits the road.

484
00:33:06,240 --> 00:33:08,240
And that's where braking comes in.

485
00:33:08,580 --> 00:33:13,080
You might be surprised to realize just how fast these trucks can actually stop.

486
00:33:13,420 --> 00:33:15,460
They have a very advanced braking system.

487
00:33:16,020 --> 00:33:18,980
The Volvo features ABS with traction control.

488
00:33:19,700 --> 00:33:24,100
We also have a new innovation for vehicle stability.

489
00:33:25,300 --> 00:33:29,760
The system works with the ABS system and traction control

490
00:33:29,761 --> 00:33:38,120
and looks at situations where the driver might be approaching an unsafe event.

491
00:33:38,800 --> 00:33:41,660
There are sensors in the vehicle that see what's happening

492
00:33:41,661 --> 00:33:46,440
as well as looking at what the driver is doing with the steering wheel, the throttle, and the brake.

493
00:33:46,441 --> 00:33:50,560
and if needed, will apply more braking,

494
00:33:50,780 --> 00:33:53,080
take throttle away so that the driver

495
00:33:53,081 --> 00:33:54,980
can continue that maneuver safely.

496
00:33:57,400 --> 00:33:59,400
The quest to make safer trucks

497
00:33:59,401 --> 00:34:00,840
has been an ongoing process.

498
00:34:02,040 --> 00:34:04,920
In the 1960s, engineers devised

499
00:34:04,921 --> 00:34:06,880
what came to be called the Jake Brake.

500
00:34:09,080 --> 00:34:11,520
The Jake Brake uses the truck's own engine

501
00:34:11,521 --> 00:34:13,680
to slow the truck down to a controlled speed

502
00:34:13,681 --> 00:34:14,640
while going downhill.

503
00:34:16,440 --> 00:34:21,440
Normally, the pistons inside the engine work to compress air

504
00:34:21,540 --> 00:34:24,440
and fuel, which causes an explosion that powers the

505
00:34:24,540 --> 00:34:26,440
vehicle.

506
00:34:26,540 --> 00:34:29,980
The jake brake slows the engine by opening the exhaust valve,

507
00:34:30,080 --> 00:34:32,380
allowing the compressed air to escape.

508
00:34:35,500 --> 00:34:40,500
During the 1970s, engineers made additional strides in safety

509
00:34:40,600 --> 00:34:43,500
as they applied ergonomics to cab design.

510
00:34:46,440 --> 00:34:54,200
They modified seats, steering wheels, and instrument panels to increase driver comfort and decrease

511
00:34:54,201 --> 00:34:55,040
driver fatigue.

512
00:34:58,000 --> 00:35:02,540
But the fuel shortages of the 70s caused the most significant change of the era.

513
00:35:03,940 --> 00:35:06,700
Fuel efficiency became the number one priority.

514
00:35:08,900 --> 00:35:11,700
One solution was aerodynamic truck design.

515
00:35:11,701 --> 00:35:19,860
Curved edges and spoilers became standard design features.

516
00:35:19,861 --> 00:35:22,540
Engine manufacturers also felt the crunch.

517
00:35:22,541 --> 00:35:29,320
In 1985, after nearly a decade of research and design, Detroit Diesel introduced the

518
00:35:29,321 --> 00:35:33,460
first electronically controlled diesel engine.

519
00:35:33,461 --> 00:35:38,180
Electronics really started a revolution in the configuration of our trucks.

520
00:35:38,181 --> 00:35:43,740
Back in the late 80s, the US government had proposed more stringent emissions requirements

521
00:35:43,741 --> 00:35:46,260
for heavy duty diesel engines.

522
00:35:46,261 --> 00:35:51,800
The only effective way to be able to meet those regulations was the addition of electronics

523
00:35:51,801 --> 00:35:56,940
to control the fuel system and in essence control the operation of our engines.

524
00:35:56,941 --> 00:36:05,040
Today's electronically controlled fuel efficient engines use massive amounts of

525
00:36:05,041 --> 00:36:10,600
torque and horsepower to pull loads up to 80,000 pounds.

526
00:36:10,601 --> 00:36:14,200
This 16-liter engine puts out 625 horsepower

527
00:36:14,201 --> 00:36:16,480
and 2,250 foot-pounds of torque.

528
00:36:16,481 --> 00:36:20,420
And torque is what gets something moving off the line.

529
00:36:20,421 --> 00:36:22,440
It gets the load moving to highway speed.

530
00:36:22,441 --> 00:36:24,840
It also allows the truck to maintain that speed going up

531
00:36:24,841 --> 00:36:25,920
a grade.

532
00:36:25,921 --> 00:36:28,440
And maintaining that speed is what allows

533
00:36:28,441 --> 00:36:32,400
the driver to get the load to the destination quicker.

534
00:36:32,401 --> 00:36:34,920
Although the most important advances in truck design

535
00:36:34,921 --> 00:36:36,900
are crowded under the hood.

536
00:36:36,901 --> 00:36:41,800
Other innovations strut their stuff proudly in the open.

537
00:36:41,801 --> 00:36:44,700
The Volvo VT880 really brings together

538
00:36:44,701 --> 00:36:46,380
a blend of aerodynamics.

539
00:36:46,381 --> 00:36:49,500
For example, you see that in the headlamp assembly,

540
00:36:49,501 --> 00:36:52,360
which is designed to keep air moving very smoothly

541
00:36:52,361 --> 00:36:53,740
over the surface.

542
00:36:53,741 --> 00:36:57,080
But it also brings in that beefy, muscular look

543
00:36:57,081 --> 00:37:00,540
that owner-operators want, the large forward hood.

544
00:37:00,541 --> 00:37:03,440
So it's a very effective blend to give the look

545
00:37:03,441 --> 00:37:05,920
and still provide maximum aerodynamics.

546
00:37:05,921 --> 00:37:07,540
Aerodynamics means fuel economy.

547
00:37:07,541 --> 00:37:11,100
Fuel economy means profitability.

548
00:37:11,101 --> 00:37:13,740
The profitable multi-billion dollar truck industry

549
00:37:13,741 --> 00:37:16,080
is banking on computer controlled collision

550
00:37:16,081 --> 00:37:19,340
avoidance systems and driverless technology

551
00:37:19,341 --> 00:37:21,640
to help pave the way to the truck of the future.

552
00:37:25,000 --> 00:37:28,480
Today's short haul truck drivers lose valuable time,

553
00:37:28,481 --> 00:37:30,920
waiting an average of 33 hours a week

554
00:37:30,921 --> 00:37:34,520
while their cargo is loaded and unloaded.

555
00:37:34,521 --> 00:37:37,080
Trucks will return on Modern Marvels.

556
00:37:42,680 --> 00:37:46,820
As the strength, speed, and safety of trucks have developed,

557
00:37:46,821 --> 00:37:48,420
our dependence upon them has grown.

558
00:37:51,420 --> 00:37:54,200
Modern life revolves around a steady stream of goods

559
00:37:54,201 --> 00:37:57,700
and raw materials being delivered on time around the globe.

560
00:38:00,920 --> 00:38:04,660
Keeping track of the millions of trucks on our highways has become a vital necessity.

561
00:38:07,180 --> 00:38:12,600
San Diego-based Qualcomm tracks over 335,000 trucks worldwide

562
00:38:12,601 --> 00:38:16,460
and receives millions of messages and position reports every day.

563
00:38:20,080 --> 00:38:22,980
The network management center receives the messages,

564
00:38:23,600 --> 00:38:27,460
along with the truck's location, via global positioning satellites.

565
00:38:27,461 --> 00:38:39,826
The truck's delivery time, location, and driver's messages are then relayed to the truck company, helping to avoid traffic congestion and decrease loading and

566
00:38:39,827 --> 00:38:41,000
unloading time.

567
00:38:45,500 --> 00:38:52,460
In its ongoing effort to maximize efficiency, the industry has pioneered several innovations in truck maintenance.

568
00:38:52,461 --> 00:39:01,360
numerous onboard sensors monitor the truck's engine brakes suspension and other vital components

569
00:39:01,361 --> 00:39:08,780
we can take that type of information and through satellite or cellular communications

570
00:39:08,781 --> 00:39:15,980
feed that information from the truck back to the maintenance repair manager back at the home base

571
00:39:15,981 --> 00:39:22,300
so long before the the truck and the driver are stranded along the road we have the capability

572
00:39:22,301 --> 00:39:26,460
to be able to forecast that in the next weeks, the next month,

573
00:39:26,980 --> 00:39:30,120
a particular system or a particular part is going to need service

574
00:39:30,121 --> 00:39:31,800
or it's going to need repair.

575
00:39:34,180 --> 00:39:36,440
So what's next in advanced truck design?

576
00:39:37,480 --> 00:39:39,280
Volvo is testing new technologies

577
00:39:39,281 --> 00:39:41,920
using this vehicle as a rolling research lab.

578
00:39:44,840 --> 00:39:47,180
The advanced engineering department within Volvo

579
00:39:47,181 --> 00:39:50,480
does build these things for the purpose of evaluating technologies

580
00:39:50,481 --> 00:39:54,040
and getting customer feedback as to whether a particular technology

581
00:39:54,041 --> 00:39:57,080
is worth pursuing to take to production or not.

582
00:39:58,400 --> 00:40:00,920
Researchers and technicians are currently testing

583
00:40:00,921 --> 00:40:03,380
a computerized wireless communication system

584
00:40:03,381 --> 00:40:07,160
that may save freight truckers from time-consuming inspection stops.

585
00:40:07,420 --> 00:40:11,780
The purpose is this truck will inspect itself with electronic sensors,

586
00:40:12,000 --> 00:40:15,260
and then as we approach a weigh station as a for instance,

587
00:40:15,960 --> 00:40:18,640
then this truck will tell the weigh station, I'm okay.

588
00:40:18,641 --> 00:40:20,180
and the waste station will say,

589
00:40:20,600 --> 00:40:22,480
this truck's okay, gets a green light,

590
00:40:22,620 --> 00:40:25,020
the trucker goes right on past without having to stop,

591
00:40:25,320 --> 00:40:27,560
there's two-some million inspections every year,

592
00:40:27,700 --> 00:40:29,320
and this truck will inspect itself

593
00:40:29,321 --> 00:40:31,060
and broadcast that inspection.

594
00:40:32,780 --> 00:40:35,620
As new generations of trucks inspect themselves,

595
00:40:36,240 --> 00:40:38,160
they'll also be able to help their drivers

596
00:40:38,161 --> 00:40:40,020
inspect what often eludes their view.

597
00:40:40,980 --> 00:40:42,460
In any truck this size,

598
00:40:42,560 --> 00:40:45,060
there is an area that's commonly called the blind spot.

599
00:40:45,460 --> 00:40:47,840
That blind spot is a dangerous place

600
00:40:47,841 --> 00:40:52,500
for a small vehicle such as a small passenger car to drive because the driver cannot see him.

601
00:40:53,200 --> 00:40:59,640
So in an attempt to try to get visibility of that blind spot, we've added this camera here.

602
00:41:00,200 --> 00:41:03,121
If I turn on my right turn signal, there's our audio man.

603
00:41:03,122 --> 00:41:05,120
I can't see him from where I'm sitting,

604
00:41:05,220 --> 00:41:07,580
but I can see him very clearly from that blind spot camera.

605
00:41:08,860 --> 00:41:13,940
Forward-facing radar is another tool commonly used today to help truck drivers avoid collisions.

606
00:41:13,941 --> 00:41:20,500
If your reaction time has been slowed by a full day on the road,

607
00:41:21,100 --> 00:41:23,560
it'll help you steer clear of slow-moving cars

608
00:41:23,561 --> 00:41:25,980
or those making erratic lane changes.

609
00:41:27,640 --> 00:41:32,240
The Doppler radar, similar to what your local weatherman may show,

610
00:41:32,840 --> 00:41:36,420
senses speed rather than just the image.

611
00:41:36,660 --> 00:41:38,700
If a vehicle pulls directly in front of the truck,

612
00:41:39,420 --> 00:41:41,740
this allows the computer then to calculate

613
00:41:41,741 --> 00:41:43,580
what is the safe following distance,

614
00:41:43,581 --> 00:41:45,840
the vehicle will automatically slow down

615
00:41:45,841 --> 00:41:47,940
to maintain that safe following distance.

616
00:41:48,580 --> 00:41:50,140
Once that traffic clears,

617
00:41:50,480 --> 00:41:53,320
the vehicle will zoom the previous set speed.

618
00:41:55,060 --> 00:41:58,860
Modern technology has made the driver's job safer and easier.

619
00:41:59,660 --> 00:42:01,820
But trucks of the future may take the driver

620
00:42:01,821 --> 00:42:02,900
right out of the equation.

621
00:42:06,200 --> 00:42:08,900
These two prototype vehicles are being developed

622
00:42:08,901 --> 00:42:10,000
by Daimler Chrysler.

623
00:42:11,000 --> 00:42:13,420
The first truck is operated conventionally.

624
00:42:13,580 --> 00:42:16,880
The second is electronically linked to the first.

625
00:42:18,560 --> 00:42:20,960
In this high-tech version of Follow the Leader,

626
00:42:21,620 --> 00:42:26,400
an infrared camera in the second truck tracks a target painted on the back of the first vehicle.

627
00:42:29,320 --> 00:42:33,680
The second vehicle drives itself with the information provided by the lead truck.

628
00:42:36,980 --> 00:42:40,920
In recent years, the Pentagon has been developing its own driverless vehicles

629
00:42:40,921 --> 00:42:44,000
with one goal in mind, saving lives.

630
00:42:44,960 --> 00:42:48,040
Historically, slow-moving military supply convoys

631
00:42:48,041 --> 00:42:51,120
have proven to be among an army's most vulnerable targets.

632
00:42:52,440 --> 00:42:54,780
Today's strategists hope that unmanned trucks

633
00:42:54,781 --> 00:42:57,620
will one day relieve soldiers of this hazardous duty.

634
00:43:00,100 --> 00:43:03,120
Oshkosh's Teramax is a thinking machine

635
00:43:03,121 --> 00:43:06,200
that gives new meaning to the term military intelligence.

636
00:43:10,920 --> 00:43:17,260
Equipped with an autonomous guidance system, the Terramax can identify obstacles, then

637
00:43:17,261 --> 00:43:27,100
decide whether to go around or over them, and at what speed, all without any human input.

638
00:43:27,101 --> 00:43:35,740
State-of-the-art unmanned vehicles are truly the trucks of the future.

639
00:43:35,741 --> 00:43:38,860
In today's electronic fast-paced society,

640
00:43:38,861 --> 00:43:41,500
trucks might seem like old world technology.

641
00:43:44,420 --> 00:43:47,120
But their ability to handle almost any load

642
00:43:47,121 --> 00:43:49,780
and deliver goods almost anywhere

643
00:43:49,781 --> 00:43:52,040
makes them an integral part of modern life.

644
00:43:53,520 --> 00:43:55,860
And that means we'll always have a need for smarter,

645
00:43:55,861 --> 00:43:58,460
more capable, more fuel efficient trucks

646
00:43:58,461 --> 00:44:02,240
and smarter, more capable drivers.

647
00:44:02,241 --> 00:44:04,140
We will see this continue into the future,

648
00:44:04,141 --> 00:44:06,980
of technology and skill and beauty

649
00:44:07,080 --> 00:44:10,080
to bring your goods to your door.

650
00:44:10,180 --> 00:44:12,920
Our love affair with the world's brawniest vehicles

651
00:44:13,020 --> 00:44:17,080
just keeps on trucking, an indelible fusion of man

652
00:44:17,180 --> 00:44:18,080
and man.

